{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that accepting Popper's defense costs the ability to claim preferences track 'verisimilitude' in any formally rigorous sense is factually incorrect regarding Popper's own terms. Popper explicitly developed a qualitative (non-quantitative) definition of verisimilitude in 'Conjectures and Refutations' and later works specifically to maintain that better-corroborated theories can be rationally preferred as closer to the truth, even after the Miller-Tichý refutation of his earlier quantitative attempts. By asserting that the framework forces a retreat to 'purely procedural conventionalism' that cannot guarantee progress toward truth, the target constructs a straw man of Popper's mature position, which retains a realist commitment to truth-likeness as the regulative ideal guiding the preference, distinct from probability but not devoid of truth-content implications.","premise":"Popper maintained a qualitative concept of verisimilitude to justify preferring better-corroborated theories as closer to the truth, despite rejecting quantitative measures.","successor_question":"Does the target's characterization of Popper's 'cost' ignore his explicit distinction between quantitative and qualitative verisimilitude in his later writings?","target":"c839dd06f8ef5476da943f222b4cd85490a0660c8db5248cd25e2f0d510429c8"}]}